.TH LD09 1 2026-11-10 "bcc09 0.11.2" "bcc09 Manual"
.SH NAME
ld09 \- linker for the Motorola 6809
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ld09
.RB [ \-MZdimstvy [ \- ]]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RB [ \-T\c
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-D\c
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-H\c
.IR size ]
.RB [ \-S
.IR script ]
.RB [ \-A
.IR symbol = value ]
.RB [ \-L\c
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-C\c
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-l\c
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-O\c
.IR file ]
.IR file ...
.br
.B ld09 \-P
.RB [ \-di ]
.RB [ \-T\c
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-D\c
.IR address ]
.RB [ \-H\c
.IR size ]
.br
.B ld09 \-r \-o
.I archive
.IR object ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ld09
links object files from
.BR as09 (1),
and members of libraries (archives made by
.BR ar09 (1)),
into an executable: an a.out file, which
.BR objcopy09 (1)
turns into what a machine loads.
It is dev86's ld86 for the 6809; the compiler driver
.BR bcc09 (1)
runs it.
.PP
.B ld09
loads only the modules that something refers to, from the entry
symbols (see
.B ENTRY
in
.BR as09 (1))
on: an object file named on the command line, like a library's
member, is left out if nothing needs it.
A common block (an uninitialized variable from the compiler) that
another module has too is no reason to load that module; a common
block's storage comes with a loaded module that has one, as large as
the largest of any module's.
A library is searched only for what is undefined when it is read, so
libraries come after the files that use them.
.PP
A linker script says where everything goes: which parts of which
modules go into each output section, where the sections are, and
symbols to define.
Without
.BR \-S ,
.B ld09
uses a built-in default script, which gives the layout under SEGMENTS;
.B \-P
prints it, as a start for one's own.
See LINKER SCRIPTS.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
The executable (a.out by default).
.TP
.BI \-T address
The address the code is linked at (0 by default).
.TP
.BI \-D address
The address the data is linked at, rather than straight after the
code; it makes the data separate
.RB ( \-i ).
.TP
.BI \-H size
Room after the BSS, for the heap (or OS-9's stack: see
.BR objcopy09 (1)).
.IP
The addresses and the size are hexadecimal, with or without a
.B 0x
(so
.B \-T3000
and
.B \-T0x3000
are the same).
With a script,
.B \-T
and
.B \-D
move its sections named
.B .text
and
.BR .data .
.TP
.B \-i
Separate code and data: the data is linked at 0 (or at
.BR \-D 's
address) instead of after the code, as OS-9's programs reach their data
through a register.
.TP
.BI \-S " script"
Link as the linker script in the file
.I script
says, rather than as the default script does.
.TP
.BI \-A " symbol" = value
Define
.IR symbol ,
as if the script began with
.IB symbol " = " value ;\c
\&: the script can use it, and it takes the place of the script's
.B PROVIDE
of the same symbol.
.I value
is an expression, written as in a script.
.TP
.B \-P
Print the default script, for the
.BR \-T ,
.BR \-D ,
.BR \-H ,
.B \-i
and
.B \-d
given, and exit.
Linking with it
.RB ( \-S )
gives the same executable as linking without a script.
No files are needed.
.TP
.B \-Z
Make input that the script doesn't place an error (see Orphans).
.TP
.BI \-l name
Link the library
.RI lib name .a,
from the library directories.
.TP
.BI \-L dir
Search
.I dir
for libraries (and start-up files) too.
.TP
.BI \-C name
Link the start-up file
.RI crt name .o,
from the library directories, first.
.TP
.BI \-O file
Link the library
.IR file ,
from the library directories.
.TP
.B \-s
Leave the symbols out of the executable.
.TP
.B \-y
Write each symbol's whole name, in the 8-character entries of an a.out
symbol table and the entries after them (the default;
.B \-y\-
cuts names to 8 characters).
.TP
.B \-d
Write the code and data alone, with no a.out header (and no symbols).
.TP
.B \-m
Print the modules linked.
.TP
.B \-M
Print the layout (each output section, with its address, size and load
address, and the input sections in it), then the symbols linked.
.TP
.B \-t
Trace the files and modules as they are read.
.TP
.B \-r
Make an archive of the objects, for
.BR ar09 (1)
or a later link, rather than linking them (with
.B \-o
required).
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version (as in
.BR "ld09 0.11.2" )
on standard output, and exit with status 0.
.PP
A flag option is turned off by following it with
.BR \- ,
as in
.BR \-y\- .
.SH SEGMENTS
This is the default script's layout.
Of the 16 segments
.RB ( LOC
0 to 15 in
.BR as09 (1)),
0 and 1 are the code, placed at the text address.
The data follows the code, or is at the data address
.RB ( \-D ,
or 0 with
.BR \-i ,
after the code is padded to a multiple of 16 bytes): segment 2 (the
direct page, moved to the start of the next 256-byte page if it would
cross one), then 3 (the data, then the BSS) to 15.
Each segment is followed by its common blocks
.RB ( COMM ,
each rounded up to a multiple of 4 bytes), and each module's part of a
segment is padded to a multiple of 4 bytes.
The initialized data ends with the last segment that has any; the
common blocks after it are the a.out's BSS, not in the file.
.PP
.B ld09
defines symbols for the program to find its parts:
.B __btext
and
.B __etext
(the code's start and end),
.B __bdata
and
.B __edata
(the data's),
.B __end
(the BSS's end),
.B __heap_top
(the end plus
.BR \-H 's
size),
.BI __seg n DL
and
.BI __seg n DH
(segment
.IR n 's
start and end,
.I n
a hexadecimal digit),
.BI __seg n CL
and
.BI __seg n CH
(its common blocks').
.BR __btext ,
.BR __etext ,
.BR __bdata ,
.B __edata
and
.B __end
are always in the executable's symbol table (unless
.BR \-s ),
where
.BR objcopy09 (1)
finds the load addresses.
.SH LINKER SCRIPTS
A script is a list of commands.
Comments are written
.B /* ... */
or start with
.BR # .
For example, a program in ROM at $E000, with its initialized data
stored in the ROM after the code but run in RAM at $0080 (where the
start-up code copies it), and the vector table at $FFF0:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ENTRY(reset)
OUTPUT_FORMAT(srec)

MEMORY
{
    ROM (rx) : ORIGIN = 0xE000, LENGTH = 0x1FF0
    VEC (r)  : ORIGIN = 0xFFF0, LENGTH = 16
    RAM (rw) : ORIGIN = 0x0080, LENGTH = 128
}

SECTIONS
{
    .text :
    {
        *(LOC0 LOC1)
    } > ROM = 0xFF

    .data :
    {
        __data_start = .;
        *(LOC3)
        __data_end = .;
    } > RAM AT> ROM

    .bss (NOLOAD) :
    {
        *(COMMON)
    } > RAM

    .vectors :
    {
        KEEP(vectors.o(LOC2))
    } > VEC

    __stack_top = ORIGIN(RAM) + LENGTH(RAM) - 1;
    __data_load = LOADADDR(.data);

    ASSERT(SIZEOF(.vectors) == 16, "vector table must be 16 bytes")
}
.fi
.RE
.SS Commands
.TP
.BI ENTRY( symbol )
Start the program at
.IR symbol ,
before any
.B ENTRY
in the object files.
.TP
.BI OUTPUT_FORMAT( format )
.B aout
(the default);
.BR binary ,
the code and data alone as with
.BR \-d ,
covering the load addresses from the lowest to the highest used;
.BR srec ,
Motorola S-records; or
.BR ihex ,
Intel hex.
S-records and Intel hex have one run of records for each section
written, at its load address, without the gaps between sections, and
the entry point in the end record; the S-records' header holds the
output file's name.
.TP
.BI MEMORY " { " "name " [( attributes )] " : ORIGIN = " "expr" ", LENGTH = " "expr ... " }
Define memory regions, such as ROM and RAM.
The attributes, such as
.BR rx ,
are only shown in the
.B \-M
layout.
.TP
.BI FILL( byte )
The byte for gaps, where a section doesn't set its own (0 by default).
.TP
.BI ASSERT( expression ", \(dq" message \(dq)
Stop with
.I message
if
.I expression
is 0 once the layout is complete.
The modules that define its symbols are linked.
.TP
.BI "INCLUDE \(dq" file \(dq
Read another script here (also inside
.BR SECTIONS ).
.TP
.BI SECTIONS " { ... }"
Output sections and symbol assignments, in order.
.TP
.IB symbol " = " expression ;
Define a symbol.
.TP
.BI PROVIDE( symbol " = " expression );
Define it only if a linked module imports it and no module defines it.
.TP
.BI HIDDEN( symbol " = " expression );
Define a symbol that only the script can use.
.SS Output sections
.nf
\fIname\fP [\fIaddress\fP] [(NOLOAD)] : [AT(\fIlma\fP)] [ALIGN(\fIn\fP)] [SUBALIGN(\fIn\fP)]
    { \fIstatements\fP } [> \fIregion\fP] [AT> \fIregion\fP] [= \fIfill\fP]
.fi
.PP
Without an
.IR address ,
a section placed in a region
.RI ( "> region" )
starts at the region's next free address, and another starts at the
current address
.RB ( . ),
where the section before it ended.
A section that doesn't fit in its region is an error.
.PP
A section is stored at its load address, which is its address unless
.B AT
gives another, or
.B AT>
allocates it from a region: initialized data that runs in RAM, for
one, can be stored in ROM after the code, and copied by the start-up
code.
Separate load addresses need the
.BR binary ,
.B srec
or
.B ihex
format.
.PP
A
.B NOLOAD
section takes up addresses but isn't written.
It may only hold space reserved with
.B RMB
and common blocks: a module's initialized data, or
.B BYTE
or
.BR WORD ,
in one is an error, since it would be lost.
.B = fill
sets the byte for the gaps in the section.
.B ALIGN
rounds the start up to a multiple of
.IR n .
.B SUBALIGN
pads each input section in it to a multiple of
.I n
bytes.
.PP
The statements in a section, in order:
.TP
.IB file ( sections )
Add the matching input sections.
.TP
.BI ONEPAGE " { statements }"
Keep the contents in one 256-byte page (as for a direct page): if they
would end on a later page than they start, they move to the start of the
next page.
.TP
.IB symbol " = " expression ";\fR, \fP" PROVIDE ", " HIDDEN
Define symbols, often with
.BR . ,
the current address.
.TP
.BI ". = " expression ;
Move the current address forward, filling the gap with the fill byte.
.TP
.BI KEEP( file ( sections ))
Add input sections as
.IB file ( sections )
does, and link their modules even if nothing refers to them.
.TP
.BI BYTE( expression "); WORD(" expression );
Put a byte, or a 16-bit word (high byte first), in the section.
.TP
.BI FILL( byte );
Use
.I byte
for the gaps that follow.
.TP
.BI ASSERT( expression ", \(dq" message \(dq);
As at the top level, but with
.B .
as here.
.SS Input sections
Each linked module has two input sections for each segment
.IR n :
.BI LOC n\c
, its code and data, and
.BI COMMON n\c
, its common blocks.
.B .text
means
.BR LOC0 ,
.B .data
means
.B LOC3
(which also holds as09's
.BR .BSS ),
and
.B COMMON
means all the common blocks.
.PP
.I file
matches the object file's name, with or without its directory; a
library's members are named
.IB library : member\c
\&.
Both
.I file
and the section names may have the wildcards
.B *
and
.BR ? .
Each input section goes to the first statement that matches it.
A statement adds its input sections in the order of the modules, and
each module's in the order of the segments.
.PP
A statement can start with
.BI EXCLUDE_FILE( "file ..." )\c
, naming files (patterns, as above) that it doesn't take input from,
also inside
.BR KEEP :
.B EXCLUDE_FILE(vectors.o) *(LOC0 LOC1)
takes segments 0 and 1 of every module but
.BR vectors.o ,
so that a later section can place it.
.SS Orphans
Input that no statement matches is added, with a warning, at the end of
the last output section for its kind (segments 0 and 1, or the others)
that takes input from all files
.RB ( * ,
without
.BR EXCLUDE_FILE ),
or else the last section for its kind.
.B \-Z
makes it an error.
.SS Expressions
Expressions have C's operators, and numbers written as in C (with
.B 0x
for hexadecimal, a leading 0 for octal), or with
.B $
for hexadecimal; a
.B K
after a number multiplies it by 1024.
The functions are
.BI ADDR( section )\c
,
.BI SIZEOF( section )\c
,
.BI LOADADDR( section )\c
,
.BI ALIGN( n )
(the current address rounded up),
.BI ALIGN( expression ", " n )\c
,
.BI DEFINED( symbol )\c
,
.BI ORIGIN( region )\c
,
.BI LENGTH( region )\c
,
.B MAX
and
.BR MIN .
A symbol may be used before it is defined: the linker repeats the layout
until nothing changes.
.SS Symbols
The symbols a script defines are absolute, and satisfy imports from any
segment.
A plain assignment to a symbol that a module also defines is an error.
.PP
Where a script doesn't assign them,
.B ld09
provides
.B __btext
and
.B __etext
(the a.out text's start and end),
.B __bdata
(its data's start),
.B __edata
(the end of the initialized data) and
.B __end
(the end of the data, BSS included), and writes them in the symbol
table, for
.BR objcopy09 (1).
It also provides
.BI __seg n DL\c
,
.BI __seg n DH\c
,
.BI __seg n CL
and
.BI __seg n CH
if a module imports them: the start and end of where segment
.IR n 's
input (or its common blocks) went, which must be in one place.
.PP
So a script for a C program needn't define anything for the C library.
Its start-up code clears the memory from
.B __edata
to
.B __end
(so only uninitialized data, such as the common blocks, goes there), and
calls the init functions, the addresses in segment 5, from
.B __seg5DL
to
.BR __seg5DH :
segment 5 needs an input statement of its own, as
.B *(LOC5)
(a statement adds its input module by module, so in
.B *(LOC3 LOC5)
each module's segment 5 would follow its segment 3).
.PP
For each output section, the linker also provides
.BI __ name _start\c
,
.BI __ name _end
and
.BI __ name _load
(its load address), with the name's leading dot dropped, if a module
imports them: start-up code can copy
.B .data
from
.B __data_load
to
.BR __data_start ,
for one.
.SS a.out output
The output sections up to the first one that can take input from
segments 2 to 15 are the a.out's text, and the rest its data.
The executable has separate code and data if the data doesn't start
where the text ends.
Uninitialized data at the end of the data, such as a
.B NOLOAD
section, is the a.out's BSS: it isn't in the file.
The a.out header's total memory reaches
.B __heap_top
if the script sets it.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A symbol defined in two linked modules is reported with a warning, as in
\(lqdup redefined in file libx.a(x.o); using definition in prog.o\(rq: the
first definition read is used.
A library member that isn't linked doesn't count, so a program can have
its own version of a library function.
.PP
Input that a script doesn't place is reported with a warning, as in
\(lqsegs.o(LOC5) isn't placed by the script; adding it to .data\(rq.
Initialized data in a module's segment that a script puts in a
.B NOLOAD
section is an error, reported once for each module and segment, as in
\(lqnoload.o: LOC4 has initialized data, but .bss is NOLOAD\(rq.
A script's errors name its file and line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if the link succeeded; 1 after an error such as an undefined symbol;
2 after an error that stops the link at once, such as a file that can't
be read, or a script's error.
.SH EXAMPLES
A program for a monitor, loaded at $4000:
.PP
.RS
.nf
ld09 \-T4000 \-o prog.out \-L/usr/share/bcc09 \-C0 prog.o \-lc \-lsa
.fi
.RE
.PP
The default script, as a start for one's own, and a link with it
(through
.BR bcc09 (1),
which passes the script with
.BR \-X ):
.PP
.RS
.nf
ld09 \-P \-T3000 > prog.ld
bcc09 \-Me \-o prog \-X\-Sprog.ld prog.c
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bcc09 (1),
.BR as09 (1),
.BR ar09 (1),
.BR objdump09 (1),
.BR objcopy09 (1)
.PP
The bcc09 User's Manual
.RI ( manual.pdf ).
.SH HISTORY
Derived from
.B ld86
in Bruce Evans' C compiler, as the dev86 package has it.
The linker scripts come from
.B ld01
in the mc6801-tools project.
